   "claims": [
    "This formula was derived in 1919 and is still widely used today to estimate BMR.",
    "Mifflin St Jeor Equation Due to the finding that the Harris-Benedict Equations overestimated BMR by about 5%, new predictive equations have been created.",
    "The Katch-McArdle equation was developed to include lean body mass when calculating BMR to better estimate resting metabolism.[6] This involves one more step in the calculation since you need to know your lean body mass.",
    "BMR = 370 + (21.6 × lean body mass [kg]) The formulas used in the BMR calculator are the Mifflin St Jeor, Harris-Benedict equation, & Katch-McArdle equation."
   ],
